{"author":"Rebar Bot","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"Stopped on this bug: it needs the policy decision held by cloistered-decomposed-nymph (still open, no decision recorded). No code was changed.\n\nRoot cause (confirmed from the Windows sweep logs at b4e0d01b649): both checks read POSIX mode bits that Windows does not implement. `src/rebar/opcert_service/keyprov.py:111` rejects `st.st_mode & 0o077`; Windows reports every file as `0o666` (dirs `0o777`), so every key is rejected (`OpcertKeyError ... 0600 or stricter (group/other bits set: mode 0o666)`, 30 tests in 7 modules). `infra/scripts/cleanup_comment_echo_jira.py` refuses its artifact directory the same way (`CleanupError: artifact directory must not be group/world accessible`, 17 tests). On Windows, file confidentiality is set by the DACL, which `os.stat` does not expose. `chmod(0o600)` in `keyprov._write_copy` also changes nothing there except the read-only bit, so a key copy inherits the parent directory's ACL.\n\nWhy this is a policy call rather than an implementation detail: an honest Windows equivalent has to decide which principals may hold access to a signing key. A user-profile file normally carries ACEs for the owner, SYSTEM and BUILTIN\\Administrators; whether those are acceptable, and whether rebar must also write a protected DACL on key copies, is exactly the question cloistered-decomposed-nymph asks. Skipping the check on Windows would silently drop the guarantee, which this bug's brief rules out.\n\nOptions:\n(1) Owner-only DACL check, OpenSSH-for-Windows rule: accept a key whose DACL grants access only to the file owner, SYSTEM and BUILTIN\\Administrators; reject any other ACE (Users, Authenticated Users, Everyone). `_write_copy` sets a protected owner-only DACL. Needs ctypes `GetNamedSecurityInfoW`/`SetNamedSecurityInfoW` code and Windows-only tests that add an `Everyone:R` ACE with `icacls` and expect rejection. Verifiable only through post-merge Windows sweeps.\n(2) Declare the op-cert service and the cleanup script Linux-only: refuse to start on Windows with an explicit error naming the missing guarantee (fail closed), and skip their tests on Windows with that reason. No key is ever loaded with an unchecked ACL.\n(3) Document reliance on the user profile's default ACL: skip the mode check on Windows and record the residual risk in the docs.\n\nDecision needed on cloistered-decomposed-nymph; this bug then implements the chosen option."},"env_id":"eea68b7f-6697-488b-ac52-744e2ed8e96f","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1795038945691453814,"uuid":"28effb22-d340-49b2-829c-de7f25f32d54"}